// The gunbc-served dashboard instantiation (docs/plans/gunbc-served-dashboard-design.md; belt B,
// parent-approved 2026-07-20). H = RoadmapServeHandler: every row is a DynamicRoute whose handler
// calls the SAME authority the static-site artifacts call (roadmap_page, roadmap_static_site) — the
// artifact table and this serve table are two projections of one authority set, never two copies of
// content, and every body is computed at request time so the served page is the LIVE roadmap, not a
// deploy-frozen snapshot (the section-3 dual-representation cost this module exists to remove).
// Dispatch: POST /dispatch/NODE_ID invokes gunbc.roadmap_belt_actuate.belt_dispatch_node IN-PROCESS
// — the single dispatch authority (#6914), no emitted JS, no subprocess exec, no env-var channel.
// The launch button that POSTs here is transport-agnostic and lands with belt A in roadmap_page
// (lane agreement 2026-07-20): the page never knows which backend answers.

data roadmap_serve_interpreted_scaffold: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: RealizationDispatch,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.roadmap_serve",
    decl_name: "roadmap_serve_handle",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

// ROADMAP section-4 rules a NEW long-running interpreted workload a review reject, routed through
// emit-on-demand. gunbc serve under the tree-walker is exactly that workload, so it lands as this
// DECLARED scaffold: dissolution = emit-on-demand of the serve closure (emit -> build -> run
// native, artifact shared by content-hash — the same lane as the S1 parse census receipt). Interim
// cost is bounded and strictly better than the belt-A alternative it supersedes: A pays a full CLI
// startup (load + compile + resolve) per dispatch click; this process pays it once at bind time.

// THE SOCKET SEAM WAS RESTORED ON 2026-08-19, AND THIS ROW RECORDS WHY THAT IS A REPAIR RATHER THAN
// NEW DEBT. #8286 deleted the cli_run driver surface and took `serve` and `converge` out as ONE
// population; the two are not the same case, and deleting them together is what made it wrong.
// Converge has a successor that EXISTS -- the modeled convergence spine, fleet_converge_timer ->
// fleet_converge_apply -> host_effect_realize -- so its verb can refuse and route there. Serve's
// declared successor is the scaffold ABOVE, roadmap_serve_interpreted_scaffold, dissolving to
// emit-on-demand of the serve closure, and it was never built. So the deletion removed a live
// outward-facing production route with no minimal Y able to hold the boundary, which is precisely
// the case DESIGN section 3 carves out: a gap-intolerant boundary keeps the staged form, and where
// no Y can hold it at all X stays but stays FROZEN. What the restore does is put X back and freeze
// it.
//
// MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED, because the cost of the repair turned on one fact. The retained engine
// still evaluates this module completely: roadmap_serve_witness_test runs 16/16 green against a
// binary built from the post-deletion tree, covering route selection, 404 and 501 folding, and
// dispatch-refuses-before-effects. What #8286 removed was the SOCKET SEAM only -- accept, minimal
// HTTP/1.1 parse, response write -- which its own surviving doc comment still described after the
// code was gone. The deletion left the prose and took the machinery, so the restore is a quarry
// revert into comments that already marked the insertion points, not new authoring.
//
// NO SECOND OBLIGATION IS REGISTERED, DELIBERATELY. The scaffold above already binds
// roadmap_serve_handle and already names the correct terminal, so its dissolution IS this seam's
// restoration trigger; a fresh row beside it would be two obligations over one independently
// removable unit, which DESIGN section 5 forbids. FROZEN MEANS: no new options, no new verbs, no
// new routes into the interpreter, no completion work, no flag or environment gate re-opening it --
// an escape hatch is refused outright and would be a NewEscapeHatchOrAdmissionRow under
// gunbc.v1_maintenance_standing besides.
//
// THE DELETED REFUSAL'S SUBSTANTIVE CLAIM IS NOT RETRACTED. Serve remains a desired SERVICE
// OCCURRENCE that convergence should observe and reconcile rather than a command, and it cannot be
// modeled that way today because gunbc.host_effect carries only the LiveDeployApply /
// LiveDeployRetract / LiveDeployEnsureDependency triple and the runner-slot family -- no generic
// service-occurrence member exists. That absence is why BOTH bring-up paths must name a command:
// the systemd unit through gunbc.live_deploy.emit, and the tmux path through
// gunbc.roadmap_dashboard_instance dashboard_instance_serve_argv. Registering that member is
// separate work and is the thing that stops this recurring.
//
// THE ENGINE OBJECTION IS RECORDED RATHER THAN ARGUED AWAY. This does make the interpreter
// load-bearing for a capability two lanes are deleting -- the propagation consequence DESIGN
// section 3 names. It is not a NEW dependency; it is the one that stood until #8286. But if this
// route begins attracting completion work or performance work on the interpreted loop, the freeze
// has been repealed by drift, which is the standing danger gunbc.v1_maintenance_standing
// v1_seed_standing already warns about for the active-maintenance arm.

type RoadmapServeHandler
  = RoadmapDailyPageHandler
  | RoadmapFullPageHandler
  | FleetStandingPageHandler
  | RoadmapMdHandler
  | DispatchJsonHandler
  | HealthzHandler
  | DashboardInstanceHandler
  | ProviderReadinessHandler
  | BeltDispatchHandler
  | DispatchAssetHandler
  | SandboxPageHandler
  | InstrumentSandboxPageHandler
  | InstrumentTunerPageHandler
  | InstrumentTunerAssetHandler
  | InstrumentMotionPageHandler
  | InstrumentMotionAssetHandler
  | RepoAtlasPageHandler
  | SandboxAssetHandler
  | SandboxEchoHandler
  | SessionsJsonHandler
  | AttemptsJsonHandler
  | WorkflowJsonHandler
  | ObservationJsonHandler
  | BeltStopHandler
  | BeltPublishHandler
  | ApprovalBrokerCutoverHandler { route: ApprovalBrokerHandler }

// Fail-closed table construction (review 40286, the section 5 catch: the earlier serve_tpl mapped
// MalformedPathTemplate to an EMPTY template, and an empty template matches exactly '/' — so a typo
// in any route literal would have silently become a second root matcher, a widen at the very seam
// section 5 forbids). THE BUILD ITSELF NO LONGER LIVES HERE. It was this module's, monomorphic over
// this module's handler vocabulary, and the approval-broker split gave it a second instantiation —
// at which point copying it would have been section 2's duplicated work at the exact seam where a
// divergence is invisible. It is now extdeps.http.server serve_build_route_table, generic in the
// handler, beside the selection fold it has always been the same layer as; this module supplies the
// rows and consumes the typed refusal. The RED control moved with it and still runs here
// (witness_malformed_route_literal_refuses_table: a bad literal through the same build fold ->
// Refused).

// THE SERVED PATH IS ONE FACT. gunbc.roadmap_site_surface_render already declared each of these
// paths as data -- the static site, the surface-observation bundle and the deploy witnesses all
// cite those constants -- while these rows carried a second spelling of the same string. Two copies
// of a path agree until one moves, and the one that moves silently is the one nothing reads back:
// a witness spelling "/roadmap.md" reaches a 404 and asserts nothing (review 63317, and the
// operator's live check against the running instance). The rows now consume the declaration, so a
// renamed route moves the routing table, the static artifact and every witness together.
// THE ONE FACT THE SERVING PROCESS CANNOT OBSERVE, DECLARED SO IT CAN REFUSE.
//
// Route / is served from a body the BELT TICK rendered, under whatever plan that process held. The
// stored body carries no identity of the authority it came from, so a serving process handed a
// plan cannot check whether the body agrees with it -- the class is filed as
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_prerendered_body_carries_no_identity_of_its_source.
//
// That made this entry's plan parameter a constructor for silent wrongness: before the parameter
// existed no caller COULD diverge, and DESIGN 4b puts silent wrongness outside the ladder and
// forbids it outright rather than admitting it at a low rung. A note saying so is not a refusal
// (4c), so the honest arm available today is the one 5 names: refuse, never widen.
//
// The agreement is therefore a DECLARED fact of the call rather than something inferred. A caller
// whose own tick renders the daily body under the same plan says so and is served; a caller that
// cannot say so gets a located refusal on that ONE route, and every other route still answers from
// its plan. This is 4b's "outside the modeled guarantee" column handled at a declared boundary --
// observed, refused, or mitigated, never fabricated -- not a fifth rung and not a proof.

type ServedDailyBodyAuthority
  = DailyBodyRenderedUnderThisPlan
  | DailyBodyAuthorityUnknown

fn roadmap_serve_own_route_specs() -> List<ServedRouteSpec<RoadmapServeHandler>> {
  [
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: roadmap_site_daily_url_path as String, handler: RoadmapDailyPageHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: roadmap_site_full_url_path as String, handler: RoadmapFullPageHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: "/fleet", handler: FleetStandingPageHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: roadmap_site_roadmap_url_path as String, handler: RoadmapMdHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: roadmap_site_dispatch_url_path as String, handler: DispatchJsonHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: roadmap_site_health_path as String, handler: HealthzHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: "/instance.json", handler: DashboardInstanceHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: "/provider.json", handler: ProviderReadinessHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: "/sessions.json", handler: SessionsJsonHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: "/attempts.json", handler: AttemptsJsonHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: "/workflow.json", handler: WorkflowJsonHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: "/observation.json", handler: ObservationJsonHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: roadmap_dispatch_asset_path, handler: DispatchAssetHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: "/sandbox", handler: SandboxPageHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: instrument_sandbox_page_path, handler: InstrumentSandboxPageHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: instrument_tuner_page_path, handler: InstrumentTunerPageHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: instrument_tuner_asset_path, handler: InstrumentTunerAssetHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: instrument_motion_page_path, handler: InstrumentMotionPageHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: instrument_motion_asset_path, handler: InstrumentMotionAssetHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: repo_atlas_page_path, handler: RepoAtlasPageHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: GET, raw: sandbox_asset_path, handler: SandboxAssetHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: POST, raw: "/sandbox/echo", handler: SandboxEchoHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: POST, raw: "/dispatch/\{node_id\}", handler: BeltDispatchHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: POST, raw: "/publish/\{node_id\}", handler: BeltPublishHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: POST, raw: "/cleanup/\{node_id\}", handler: BeltStopHandler },
    ServedRouteSpec { method: POST, raw: "/stop/\{node_id\}", handler: BeltStopHandler },
  ]
}

// THE LIVE TABLE IS THIS MODULE'S OWN ROWS PLUS THE ROWS IT IS STILL MOUNTING FOR THE BROKER MID-
// CUTOVER, and the split into two producers is what makes stage 6 a deletion rather than an edit
// inside a list of thirty literals.
fn roadmap_serve_route_specs() -> List<ServedRouteSpec<RoadmapServeHandler>> {
  concat(roadmap_serve_own_route_specs(), roadmap_serve_approval_cutover_route_specs())
}

fn roadmap_serve_route_table_build() -> ServedRouteTableBuild<RoadmapServeHandler> {
  serve_build_route_table(specs: roadmap_serve_route_specs())
}

fn serve_text_response(status: HttpStatus, content_type: MediaType, body: String) -> ServeHttpResponse {
  ServeHttpResponse { status: status, content_type: content_type, body: body }
}

// A page render refusal is a TYPED 500 carrying the emitter's reason — never an empty page and
// never a dropped route (the EmitRejected => [] route-drop in node_http_server_emit is a silent
// widen this path deliberately does not copy; DESIGN 5). The BODY of both arms is
// roadmap_static_site.served_page_body, shared with the healthz digest map rather than spelled
// twice: healthz publishes the digest of the served surface so a deploy can prove the running
// process is serving THIS tree, and a digest taken over a body that is merely supposed to match the
// route's would be validation standing where construction was available — the check would go green
// while the route lied. Only the STATUS split lives here, because a digest has no status.

fn serve_page_response(page: Fragment) -> ServeHttpResponse {
  match try_serialize_html_source(node: page) {
    EmitOk { html: h } =>
      serve_text_response(status: 200, content_type: text_html_utf8, body: h)
    EmitRejected { reason: r } =>
      serve_text_response(
        status: 500,
        content_type: text_plain_utf8,
        body: page_render_refusal_body(reason: r),
      )
  }
}

fn serve_page_response_with_status(page: Fragment, status: HttpStatus) -> ServeHttpResponse {
  match try_serialize_html_source(node: page) {
    EmitOk { html } => serve_text_response(status: status, content_type: text_html_utf8, body: html)
    EmitRejected { reason } => serve_text_response(
      status: 500,
      content_type: text_plain_utf8,
      body: page_render_refusal_body(reason: reason),
    )
  }
}

// TOTAL per-arm status mapping over the route's outcome (DESIGN 5): every BeltSpawnOutcome arm and
// every LaunchRefusal arm has its own row in launch_refusal_http_status -- no lazy 200-or-500
// collapse. Spawned and LaunchAlreadyLive = 200 (the click achieved or already holds the desired
// state, the same idempotent-success fold the CLI exit map uses); LaunchNoActuatingInstance and
// LaunchInstanceActuationRefused = 403 (no actuating instance stands behind the request);
// LaunchNodeUnknown = 404; the state conflicts (superseded, accepted, review-only, unsized,
// contract missing, dependencies blocked, stale or conflicting session, capacity, cause not
// permitted by mode) = 409, retryable only after the state changes; the observation refusals
// (transition inhibited, revision unobserved or drifted, authority unobserved, sessions
// unobservable, liveness unobserved, gate not evaluated) = 503, a retry can succeed; SpawnFailed =
// 502 (actuation behind the server failed; the receipt carries step, detail and the admitted launch
// identity).

fn launch_refusal_http_status(r: LaunchRefusal) -> HttpStatus {
  match r {
    LaunchAlreadyLive { session_name: _ } => 200
    LaunchNoActuatingInstance { reason: _ } => 403
    LaunchInstanceActuationRefused { instance_id: _, reason: _ } => 403
    LaunchNodeUnknown => 404
    LaunchAlreadyAccepted => 409
    LaunchNodeSuperseded { by: _ } => 409
    LaunchReviewRequired => 409
    LaunchSizeMissing => 409
    LaunchExecutionContractMissing { reason: _ } => 409
    LaunchNotAgentWork { mode: _ } => 409
    LaunchDependenciesBlocked { blockers: _ } => 409
    LaunchNoCapacity { occupied: _, max_concurrent: _ } => 409
    LaunchCauseNotPermitted { cause: _, mode: _ } => 409
    LaunchStaleSession { session_name: _, detail: _ } => 409
    LaunchTransitionInhibited { reason: _ } => 409
    LaunchSessionMultiplicityConflict { session_names: _, session_count: _ } => 409
    LaunchSessionsUnobservable { reason: _ } => 503
    LaunchAuthorityUnobserved { detail: _ } => 503
    LaunchRevisionUnobserved { cause: _ } => 503
    LaunchLivenessUnobserved { session_name: _, reason: _ } => 503
    LaunchGateNotEvaluated { gate: _ } => 503
  }
}

fn belt_dispatch_status(r: BeltSpawnOutcome) -> HttpStatus {
  match r {
    Spawned { node_id: _, session_name: _, provider: _, launch: _ } => 200
    SpawnFailed { node_id: _, step: _, detail: _, provider: _, launch: _ } => 502
    SpawnNotAdmitted { node_id: _, refusal } => launch_refusal_http_status(r: refusal)
  }
}

fn belt_dispatch_response(r: BeltSpawnOutcome) -> ServeHttpResponse {
  serve_text_response(
    status: belt_dispatch_status(r: r),
    content_type: application_json_utf8,
    body: serialize_json(v: belt_spawn_outcome_json(o: r)),
  )
}

// The node_id param is bound by the route template, so an empty value is unreachable by
// construction (match_path_template refuses empty segments); the empty-check below is the typed
// backstop for that unreachable arm — a 400 refusal, never a dispatch with a fabricated id (DESIGN
// 5: an unreachable arm refuses, never fabricates).

fn serve_dispatch_handler_response_over(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  plan: RoadmapAuthorityProjection,
  params: List<PathParamBinding>,
) -> ServeHttpResponse {
  let node_id = path_param_value(params: params, name: "node_id")
  if node_id == "" {
    serve_text_response(
      status: 400,
      content_type: text_plain_utf8,
      body: "dispatch requires a node id path segment (fail-closed; empty binding is unreachable by construction)\n",
    )
  } else {
    belt_dispatch_response(
      r: belt_dispatch_node_for_instance_over(instance: instance, plan: plan, node_id: node_id),
    )
  }
}

fn serve_dispatch_handler_response_for_instance(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  params: List<PathParamBinding>,
) -> ServeHttpResponse {
  serve_dispatch_handler_response_over(
    instance: instance,
    plan: roadmap_authority_projection(),
    params: params,
  )
}

// POST /publish/NODE_ID is the publication producer's live trigger, and it exists because belt_tick
// has none. Publication is wired into that actuation pass beside verification and nothing drives
// it, so without this route the receipt would never be written in production and the row's lamp
// could never move off pending - the same never-runs defect one layer out from the one this lane
// fixed.\n\nIT IS A POST BECAUSE IT WRITES. The workflow route reads and paints; this one observes
// two remote surfaces and persists a judgment, and putting that on the read path would make the
// browser poll the thing that queries GitHub - the split serve_workflow_note already draws for
// verification.\n\nTHE STATUS COMES FROM THE OUTCOME, not from whether the handler ran. A deferral
// is 409 and a belt-side failure is 500, so a caller can tell `the attempt is not ready` from `the
// belt could not do its job` without reading prose.

fn serve_publish_handler_response_for_instance(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  params: List<PathParamBinding>,
) -> ServeHttpResponse {
  let node_id = path_param_value(params: params, name: "node_id")
  if node_id == "" {
    serve_text_response(
      status: 400,
      content_type: text_plain_utf8,
      body: "publish requires a node id path segment (fail-closed; empty binding is unreachable by construction)\n",
    )
  } else {
    let outcome = belt_publish_node_for_instance(instance: instance, node_id: node_id)
    serve_text_response(
      status: belt_publish_outcome_http_status(o: outcome),
      content_type: application_json_utf8,
      body: serialize_json(v: belt_publish_outcome_json_value(o: outcome)),
    )
  }
}

// ── THE APPROVAL ROUTES ARE MID-CUTOVER, AND THIS IS THE STAGE THEY ARE AT ───────────────────
// GET/POST /approve/\{capability\}, POST /approvals and GET /approvals/\{escalation_id\} are being
// moved out of this process into gunbc.auth.approval_broker_serve, which has its own unit, slice,
// MainPID and memory bound.
//
// THE RECEIPT IS AN OUTAGE, NOT A TIDINESS ARGUMENT (side-chat ruling 2026-09-19). Boot 15 refused
// on an approval status poll because THIS process's 18 GB heap was swapped and OOM-killed. The
// approval folds were fine; they were merely resident in a process whose job is expensive renders.
// The note further down still says what made that possible -- the approval routes consulted no
// roadmap at all -- so the only thing joining them to a roadmap render was co-tenancy, and
// co-tenancy is repaired by splitting the process, not by rendering more cheaply.
//
// WHAT LANDED HERE AND WHY IT IS ONE ARM RATHER THAN FOUR. The handler folds and the path literals
// are GONE from this module: they live once, in the broker's module and in
// gunbc.auth.approval_broker_endpoint. What remains is a single arm carrying the broker's own
// handler value, and route rows PROJECTED from the broker's own specs -- so during the cutover
// window there are two route TABLES and still exactly one authority for what an approval route is,
// where it is mounted and what it does. Copying the adapter to keep serving here would have been
// the DESIGN section 2 duplication this split exists to remove, and re-spelling the paths would
// have been the section 3 fork.
//
// WHY THE ROWS ARE STILL HERE AT ALL: the staged transaction the split was ordered under puts the
// dark deployment and the measurement BEFORE anything is repointed, and the route removal LAST
// (gunbc.auth.approval_broker_cutover). Removing them in the same change that first introduces the
// broker would take the approval loop down for the window between the two -- an outage strictly
// worse than the co-tenancy it repairs, and the reason the plan is staged rather than atomic.
//
// THE DISSOLUTION IS ONE ROW AND NOTHING ELSE, which is what makes it a mechanism rather than a
// plan someone remembers. gunbc.auth.approval_broker_cutover approval_route_ownership decides who
// the approval paths are routed to, and every derived fact reads it: whether this table projects
// the four rows, which port the deploy binds the broker to, and whether the front door carries the
// two approval mounts. At most one party is routed at every value of that row, which is the
// property an earlier arrangement asserted in prose and a later one tried to get from the port
// alone (reviews 68050 and 68075).
//
// This projection stops at ApprovalPathsOwnedByBroker. The arm below survives that flip as dead
// code for exactly one change and is deleted with it; what stays is the regression control that
// this table answers nothing for the four approval pairs.

fn roadmap_serve_approval_cutover_route_specs() -> List<ServedRouteSpec<RoadmapServeHandler>> {
  if roadmap_still_mounts_approval_routes() {
    map(approval_broker_route_specs(), spec => ServedRouteSpec {
      method: spec.method,
      raw: spec.raw,
      handler: ApprovalBrokerCutoverHandler { route: spec.handler },
    })
  } else {
    []
  }
}

fn serve_dispatch_handler_response(params: List<PathParamBinding>) -> ServeHttpResponse {
  serve_dispatch_handler_response_for_instance(
    instance: srv1_live_dashboard_instance(),
    params: params,
  )
}

fn dashboard_instance_json(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  let plan = dashboard_instance_provision_plan_rows(instance: instance)
  serialize_json(v: json_object(members: [
    json_kv(key: "schema", value: json_string(s: dashboard_instance_health_schema)),
    json_kv(key: "instance_id", value: json_string(s: instance.instance_id as String)),
    json_kv(key: "host_identity", value: json_string(s: instance.host_identity as String)),
    json_kv(
      key: "actuation",
      value: json_string(s: dashboard_actuation_posture_label(posture: instance.actuation)),
    ),
    json_kv(key: "repo_root", value: json_string(s: instance.repo_root as String)),
    json_kv(
      key: "dispatch_worktree_root",
      value: json_string(s: dashboard_instance_dispatch_worktree_root(instance: instance) as String),
    ),
    json_kv(
      key: "dispatch_attempt_state_root",
      value: json_string(s: dashboard_instance_dispatch_attempt_state_root(instance: instance) as String),
    ),
    json_kv(key: "listen_port", value: json_int(n: instance.listen_port)),
    json_kv(key: "serve_function", value: json_string(s: instance.serve_function as String)),
    json_kv(key: "dispatch_base_ref", value: json_string(s: instance.dispatch_base_ref as String)),
    json_kv(
      key: "provision_transport",
      value: json_string(s: dashboard_provision_transport_label(
        transport: instance.provision_transport,
      )),
    ),
    json_kv(
      key: "desired_provision_plan",
      value: json_array(elements: plan |> map(row => json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: "kind", value: json_string(s: row.kind as String)),
        json_kv(key: "target", value: json_string(s: row.target)),
        json_kv(key: "detail", value: json_string(s: row.detail)),
      ]))),
    ),
  ]))
}

// The /sandbox echo handler answers ANY wire arm on demand so every dispatch state is pressable
// without spawning a session (P4). It reads ?status=LABEL from the request path (uri_query_param —
// the query survives in request.path; match_path_template strips it only for routing) and maps it
// to the belt result exemplar via belt_dispatch_result_for_label, serializing the SAME
// belt_dispatch_result_json_value the real dispatch returns — so the sandbox JSON shape is
// identical to production, never a mock. Fail-closed: an unknown or missing status refuses with a
// 400 that names the valid label set, never a fabricated arm.

fn sandbox_echo_response(request: ServeHttpRequest) -> ServeHttpResponse {
  let status = uri_query_param(path: request.path, key: "status")
  match belt_dispatch_result_for_label(wire_label: status) {
    Present { value: r } =>
      serve_text_response(
        status: 200,
        content_type: application_json_utf8,
        body: serialize_json(v: belt_spawn_outcome_json(o: r)),
      )
    Absent =>
      serve_text_response(
        status: 400,
        content_type: text_plain_utf8,
        body: join([
          "unknown status label: ", status,
          " (fail-closed; valid: ", join(belt_dispatch_all_status_labels(), ", "), ")\n",
        ], ""),
      )
  }
}

// Every handler receives the FULL modeled ServeHttpRequest (review 40314: the request body was
// formerly accepted at the seam and dropped before invoke, so a body-reading POST handler would
// have silently seen the empty string — a latent widen).

// THE PLAN TRAVELS WITH THE REQUEST, exactly as the instance already does. Which plan a process
// serves is fixed by its serve entry function -- `gunbc serve` binds --entry/--function and carries
// no per-request argument -- so an entry declared elsewhere supplies its own projected plan by
// CALLING this fold, and nothing here enumerates or registers callers. The handlers that consult a
// roadmap read the parameter instead of the public carrier, so one response cannot mix two plans.
//
// WHICH HANDLERS IGNORE IT, AND THE ONE THAT ONLY LOOKS LIKE IT DOES. Assets and the sandbox echo
// consult no roadmap at all. The sessions, attempts, workflow and observation surfaces read bodies
// the belt tick already wrote, and none of them is a roadmap render.
//
// RoadmapDailyPageHandler IS a roadmap render and must not be filed with them (review 63341, which
// caught this note grouping it there). Its plan is bound EARLIER, at the tick that rendered the
// body, by gunbc.roadmap_served_observation served_observation_observe_and_write_over -- so the
// daily page is parameterized, on the other entry. The consequence a caller has to know: supplying
// a plan HERE does not reach `/`. A process serving its own plan must supply it at BOTH entries,
// the serve entry and the tick entry; supply it at only this one and `/` answers from whatever plan
// the tick that wrote the body was given, with no refusal, because a pre-rendered body carries no
// record of the plan it came from. That is a real seam between two processes, not a gap inside this
// fold, and it is stated rather than papered over: nothing here can detect the mismatch.
fn roadmap_serve_invoke_over(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  plan: RoadmapAuthorityProjection,
  daily_body: ServedDailyBodyAuthority,
  handler: RoadmapServeHandler,
  params: List<PathParamBinding>,
  request: ServeHttpRequest,
  release_revision: CommitSha,
) -> ServeHttpResponse {
  match handler {
    RoadmapDailyPageHandler =>
      match daily_body {
        DailyBodyRenderedUnderThisPlan => serve_daily_page_response_for_instance(instance: instance)
        DailyBodyAuthorityUnknown =>
          serve_text_response(
            status: 503,
            content_type: text_plain_utf8,
            body: join([
              "the daily workspace is served from a body this process did not render: the belt tick ",
              "wrote it under the plan THAT process held, and a stored body carries no identity of ",
              "the authority it came from, so agreement with the plan this entry was given cannot ",
              "be decided here. Refusing rather than answering from an unknown authority ",
              "(gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_prerendered_body_carries_no_identity_of_its_source). ",
              "Bind the same plan at the tick entry, gunbc.roadmap_served_observation ",
              "served_observation_observe_and_write_over, and declare it with ",
              "DailyBodyRenderedUnderThisPlan.\n",
            ], ""),
          )
      }
    RoadmapFullPageHandler =>
      serve_page_response(page: roadmap_page_for_projection_launch(
        proj: plan,
        launch: belt_launch_host_standing_for_page_over(instance: instance, plan: plan),
      ))
    FleetStandingPageHandler => serve_page_response(page: roadmap_fleet_page())
    RoadmapMdHandler => serve_text_response(status: 200, content_type: text_plain_utf8, body: roadmap_site_roadmap_md_body_over(plan: plan))
    DispatchJsonHandler => serve_text_response(status: 200, content_type: application_json_utf8, body: roadmap_site_dispatch_json_body_over(plan: plan))
    HealthzHandler => serve_text_response(status: 200, content_type: application_json_utf8, body: roadmap_site_healthz_body_for_release(release_revision: release_revision))
    DashboardInstanceHandler =>
      serve_text_response(
        status: 200,
        content_type: application_json_utf8,
        body: dashboard_instance_json(instance: instance),
      )
    ProviderReadinessHandler =>
      serve_text_response(
        status: 200,
        content_type: application_json_utf8,
        body: dashboard_provider_readiness_json(instance: instance),
      )
    BeltDispatchHandler =>
      serve_dispatch_handler_response_over(instance: instance, plan: plan, params: params)
    DispatchAssetHandler => serve_text_response(status: 200, content_type: text_javascript_utf8, body: dispatch_client_js_source())
    SandboxPageHandler => serve_page_response(page: sandbox_page())
    InstrumentSandboxPageHandler => serve_page_response(page: instrument_sandbox_page())
    InstrumentTunerPageHandler => serve_page_response(page: instrument_tuner_page())
    InstrumentTunerAssetHandler => serve_text_response(status: 200, content_type: text_javascript_utf8, body: instrument_tuner_client_js_source())
    InstrumentMotionPageHandler => serve_page_response(page: instrument_motion_page())
    InstrumentMotionAssetHandler => serve_text_response(status: 200, content_type: text_javascript_utf8, body: instrument_motion_client_js_source())
    RepoAtlasPageHandler => serve_page_response(page: repo_atlas_page())
    SandboxAssetHandler => serve_text_response(status: 200, content_type: text_javascript_utf8, body: sandbox_client_js_source())
    SandboxEchoHandler => sandbox_echo_response(request: request)
    SessionsJsonHandler => serve_sessions_response_for_instance(instance: instance)
    AttemptsJsonHandler => serve_attempts_response_for_instance(instance: instance)
    WorkflowJsonHandler => serve_workflow_response_for_instance(instance: instance)
    ObservationJsonHandler => served_observation_standing_response_for_instance(instance: instance)
    BeltStopHandler => serve_stop_handler_response_for_instance(instance: instance, params: params)
    BeltPublishHandler => serve_publish_handler_response_for_instance(instance: instance, params: params)
    // THE ARM DECLARES WHICH PROCESS IT IS, and that is what stops this mount being a second
    // writer. The handler folds are the broker's own, so nothing inside them can tell they were
    // reached from here; passing RoadmapProcess means the two mutating routes consult
    // gunbc.auth.approval_writer_authority and refuse the moment authority moves to the broker,
    // while this table is still mounting them for rollback (side-chat HOLD item 3: port identity is
    // not writer authority, and route code may stay only if its store effects are gated by the same
    // authority).
    ApprovalBrokerCutoverHandler { route: r } =>
      approval_broker_invoke(handler: r, params: params, request: request, process: RoadmapProcess)
  }
}

fn roadmap_serve_invoke_for_instance(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  handler: RoadmapServeHandler,
  params: List<PathParamBinding>,
  request: ServeHttpRequest,
  release_revision: CommitSha,
) -> ServeHttpResponse {
  roadmap_serve_invoke_over(
    instance: instance,
    plan: roadmap_authority_projection(),
    daily_body: DailyBodyRenderedUnderThisPlan,
    handler: handler,
    params: params,
    request: request,
    release_revision: release_revision,
  )
}

fn roadmap_serve_invoke(
  handler: RoadmapServeHandler,
